>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> non-UI business logic belongs in <em>capabilities</em>. Hosts and embedders do UI and I/O. The runtime stays one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>. The agent proposes; the runtime decides.</p>

  <h2>Capability vs host</h2>
  <p>A capability is a versioned contract plus a sandboxed WASM artifact: pricing, eligibility, consent checks, mesh codecs, overhang analysis, orientation scoring, export gates — anything that must behave the same way for every caller and leave a trace. The host (browser app, native shell, CLI, MCP client) renders UI, holds session state, and supplies the I/O the capability is allowed to use. It is not the forever home of the domain rule.</p>

  <p>If you are choosing where a rule should live, ask: would it be wrong for two hosts to disagree? If yes, put it in a capability.</p>

  <h2>Utilities are not the ceiling</h2>
  <p>The registry includes small helpers (slugify, truncate, email validate). Those teach the publish pipeline and CI gates. They are legitimate. They are <strong>not</strong> the product identity and not a size limit. Preferred publishes are business-shaped: deterministic decisions, domain transforms, policy gates. “One small capability” means <strong>one concern per package</strong>, not “keep it tiny forever.”</p>

  <h2>“Apps are not ready” is sequencing, not expressiveness</h2>
  <p>When help-wanted tickets say apps are not ready, they mean: do not open a PR that rewrites a whole product UI or ports an entire application shell into this org’s capability repos. Capability packs are in scope. Domain logic in WASM is the point. See the print-support initiative as a living example of a domain pack (capabilities only) — Announcement on the org Discussions, umbrella <code>registry#596</code>.</p>

  <h2>Deny-by-default I/O is not “leave logic in JS”</h2>
  <p>Capabilities do not get ambient filesystem or network access. Today’s common execution boundary is stdin/stdout JSON through the embedder. That limits <em>authority</em>, not <em>how rich the rule can be</em>. Putting pricing or eligibility back into React “because WASM cannot touch disk” is the wrong conclusion. Call the capability; keep the host thin.</p>

  <p>The React guide’s “app never touches Rust/WASM directly” means you go through the published embedder client. It does <strong>not</strong> mean reimplement the business rule in React.</p>

  <h2>How to author</h2>
  <p>Usual path: plain English via the Claude skill <code>traverse-capability-author</code> — interview → registry check → contract → WASM → human-reviewed PR. You do not need to write Rust for that path. Manual authoring in-repo still uses Rust→WASM under the hood. Start at <a href="/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html">How do I author a capability in plain English?</a> and the citeable snapshot at <a href="/what-is-real-today.html">what is real today</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not “Traverse is only tiny utils.”</li>
    <li>Not “leave domain logic in the host forever.”</li>
    <li>Not a promise that every OS host or every I/O shape is shipped — check <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a>.</li>
    <li>Not an invitation to invent a Python SDK or a second runtime. One shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>; Python shells out to the CLI today.</li>
  </ul>
